data.content:
# EF Core 10 Breaking Changes for Your Code

## 1. `ExecuteUpdateAsync` — `SetProperty` value must be a lambda expression

In EF Core 10, the `SetProperty(property, value)` overload taking a raw value was removed. The value argument must now be an `Expression<Func<TSource, TProperty>>`.

Your `Expression.Constant("foo")` is passed as a direct value — it needs to be wrapped in a lambda:

```csharp
// Before (EF Core 9)
Expression.Constant("foo")

// After (EF Core 10) — wrap constant in a lambda
var valueBlogParam = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b");
// ...
arguments: new Expression[]
{
    Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
        Expression.Property(blogParameter, nameof(Blog.Name)), blogParameter),
    Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
        Expression.Constant("foo"), valueBlogParam)
}
```

## 2. Complex types — default column naming changed

EF Core 10 changed the default column-naming convention for complex properties. When multiple complex properties share a type (e.g., `ShippingAddress` and `BillingAddress` both of type `Address`), columns now include the navigation name as a prefix by default (e.g., `ShippingAddress_Street`). Nested complex types likewise chain names (`Payment_BillingAddress_City`).

If you have an existing database, either **generate a new migration** or pin the old names explicitly:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b => {
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, a => {
        a.Property(x => x.Street).HasColumnName("Street");
        // ... other properties
    });
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, a => {
        a.Property(x => x.Street).HasColumnName("BillingStreet");
    });
});

// For nested: Order.Payment.BillingAddress.City
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b => {
    b.ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment, p => {
        p.ComplexProperty(pay => pay.BillingAddress, a => {
            a.Property(x => x.City).HasColumnName("Payment_BillingAddress_City");
        });
    });
});
```

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `CommandSource` parameter

EF Core 10 added a `CommandSource` parameter to the diagnostic methods. Update your implementation:

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        CommandSource commandSource)   // ← new parameter
    { /* logging */ }
}
```

---

**Summary of actions:**
| Area | What broke | Fix |
|------|-----------|-----|
| `ExecuteUpdate` expression tree | `SetProperty` value no longer accepts raw values | Wrap value in `Expression.Lambda<Func<T, TProp>>(...)` |
| Complex types | Column naming convention now prefixes with navigation name | Add explicit `HasColumnName()` or generate a migration |
| Diagnostics logger | `CommandSource` param added to interface methods | Add the new parameter to your implementation |
